 2. Pizza giveaway exposes AI agent growing pains 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A startup offered free pizza on Friday in exchange for letting its AI agent handle the ordering. After multiple attempts, I ended up with a $30 DoorDash credit, no pizza and an empty stomach.

 Why it matters: AI agents promise to take everyday tasks off our plates, but they still have to clear a basic hurdle: making a task easier than doing it ourselves.

 Driving the news: AI startup Hark advertised a $1 million giveaway, encouraging a download of Hark Pro and offering $30 in DoorDash credit as part of a promotion designed to showcase its agent's ability to order food.

 A number of customers reported experiences that were considerably less appetizing, including an investor who said it took 20 minutes , a user whose promotional credit didn't apply (who paid for the pizza out of pocket) and a startup founder who joked that she'd never worked so hard for anything. Zoom in: I repeatedly tried to get Hark to order my pizza, without success.

 I did receive the promised $30 DoorDash credit, but the agent couldn't complete the order. What they're saying: Hark acknowledged that the promotion strained its systems.

 "We're so happy the majority of people had a great night with a free pizza," a spokesperson said. "Obviously, we hate to have let anyone down and we've made updates on how we handle peak surge requests." The big picture: Hark's experience illustrates a challenge facing the broader AI agent industry.

 AI agents can navigate websites, fill out forms and complete purchases, but doing so often requires authentication, permissions and human intervention. Those extra steps can make an otherwise simple transaction more complicated and, at times, easier for a user to complete on their own. The bottom line: AI agents are getting better at doing things for us. But that doesn't necessarily mean they're making our lives easier.

 
 
